# Implementation Plan

## Phase 0: Foundation

- [x] Rust arithmetic DAG and deterministic scheduler; tests and benchmark CLI.
- [x] Apache-2.0 license, contributor and security policies.
- [x] Hosted CI and public repository approval.

## Phase 1: Evaluator (days 2–4)

- [x] Freeze reference arithmetic and public development workloads; check fixed outputs and necessary cycle bounds (#4).
- [x] Add negative controls and reference comparisons for public fixtures (#4).
- [x] Implement reviewer-side corpus validation, public fixture separation checks and digest protocol (#5).
- [x] Add a bounded input CLI and independently checked trusted baseline execution (#12).
- [ ] Independent custodian prepares, isolates, verifies and seals a real withheld corpus before candidate search (#5).

## Phase 2: Search (days 5–7)

- [ ] Freeze workload categories and baseline comparison rules; add a bounded, deterministic candidate search and content-addressed experiment ledger only after the independent corpus seal.
- [ ] Record the candidate hypothesis, parameter manifest, corpus/evaluator/RTL hashes, exact tool versions, seed, cycle counts, wall time, resources and all failures. Keep candidate worktrees isolated; evaluators remain reviewer-controlled.
- [ ] Cap each batch at 100 software, 10 RTL and two routed candidates initially; agents propose structural changes, deterministic algorithms search parameters.

## Phase 3: RTL (week 2)

- [x] Fixed single-lane SystemVerilog baseline, reference fixture comparison, reset/backpressure and malformed-input tests (#14).
- [ ] Implement an out-of-context Vivado flow for the exact Cora Z7-07S part; record clock, tool version, routed slack and utilization (#16). No candidate optimization until the independent holdout seal.
- [ ] Validate Cora Z7-07S configuration and routed Vivado timing/resource reports.
- [ ] Establish executable protocol assertions and bounded formal equivalence of arithmetic/ownership/reset/backpressure against the reference; select formal tooling after a feasibility experiment. Do not claim proofs beyond the verified bounds.
- [ ] Calibrate the software timing model against RTL traces and routed clock limits before ranking physical-performance candidates; report error and uncertainty.

## Phase 4: Hardware (week 3)

- [ ] Freeze finalist and baseline revisions; independently verify the withheld workloads and compare the same target, clock constraints, precision and input/output boundaries. Record model/RTL cycles, routed frequency and LUT/FF/BRAM/DSP usage; retain ablations and failed candidates.
- [ ] Operator-approved physical test and reproducible evidence; distinguish estimated power from measured energy.

## Phase 5: Public Alpha (week 4)

- [ ] Locked build, tests, lint, CLI reproduction and graph integrity on supported hosts.
- [ ] Review source for secrets, private data, upstream licenses and security risks.
- [ ] Prepare the initial SemVer release PR, verify Cargo version and lockfile, date the changelog entry, and obtain maintainer approval for a matching immutable tag; publish tutorial and evidence (#9).
- [ ] Validate demand with hardware researchers and small engineering teams; evaluate integration, custom optimization and support.

Target windows are planning assumptions. Release a clearly labeled simulator alpha if hardware slips; no unverified performance or market-leadership claims. Defer custom ISA, OS, ASIC and full interaction-net runtime until justified.

## Verify

### Entity: Program
file: src/lib.rs
properties: [nodes]

### Entity: Run
file: src/lib.rs
properties: [output, cycles]

### Entity: Workload
file: src/benchmarks.rs
properties: [id, program, expected_output, node_count, critical_path]

### Entity: CorpusSeal
file: scripts/verify-holdout.mjs
properties: [schema, case_count, corpus_sha256, evaluator_sha256, public_suite_sha256]

### Entity: RtlResult
file: rtl/arithmetic_engine.sv
properties: [out_value, out_nodes, out_error]
